  {% set ubigiPrimary = providerB.codes | findCode(providerB.primaryCode) %}

  <div class="content-callout">
    <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
    <p>
      Pick <strong>Holafly</strong> for a flat unlimited price and the least amount of thinking about data. Pick <strong>Ubigi</strong> if you want a structured recurring plan for a destination you visit often, or you'd rather buy from an established telecom (Ubigi's parent, Transatel, is part of NTT Group) than a travel-focused reseller. Both use the same discount code, <strong>ADAMANDLINDS</strong>, since it's ours on each.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The Core Difference: Unlimited Flat Rate vs. Metered Recurring</h2>
  <p>
    Holafly sells unlimited data as the whole product: one flat price per day, per country or region, no gigabyte counting. Ubigi sells data by the gigabyte like most providers, but it's built a genuinely different plan structure around that: alongside its "one-off" fixed-GB plans, it sells recurring monthly and annual subscriptions that auto-renew with a fresh allowance each period. A monthly Ubigi plan can't be cancelled until you've held it for three months minimum; an annual plan is paid upfront and can't be cancelled or refunded at all during that year. Holafly has no equivalent recurring product; every Holafly plan is a one-time purchase for a set number of days.
  </p>
  <p>
    Ubigi is also the more expensive option almost everywhere: $4 for 1GB in the US on Ubigi versus roughly $2 a day for genuinely unlimited data on Holafly. What Ubigi buys instead of a lower price is a different kind of company behind it: Ubigi is a brand of Transatel, an MVNO enabler running cellular connectivity since the early 2000s and now part of NTT Group, one of the largest telecom companies in the world. The same underlying network infrastructure that powers a Ubigi travel eSIM also runs the connected-car SIMs in vehicles from BMW, Toyota, Jeep, Maserati, Fiat, Alfa Romeo, Mini, Jaguar, and Land Rover.
  </p>

