      <li><strong>Ubigi:</strong> {{ providerB.primaryCode }}, {{ ubigiPrimary.discount | lower }}. <a href="/ubigi/">Full code list &rarr;</a></li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top:.75rem;margin-bottom:0;color:#63553F;">ADAMANDLINDS is our own code on both providers, which is why it's the same string in both boxes. On Holafly it covers a single eSIM (5% off) or a monthly plan (10% off); on Ubigi it's a flat 10% off any plan, one-off or recurring.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Is Holafly or Ubigi the Right Pick</h2>
  <div class="decision-grid">
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--primary">
      <h3>Go with Holafly if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You genuinely don't know how much data you'll use</li>
        <li>Heavy streaming, video calls, or hotspot use</li>
        <li>China is on the itinerary and you want the built-in VPN</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--secondary">
      <h3>Go with Ubigi if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You visit the same country often enough that a recurring monthly plan beats a fresh eSIM every trip</li>
        <li>You already drive a connected car from one of Ubigi's automaker partners and like the idea of one provider</li>
        <li>You'd rather buy from an established MVNO than a travel-focused reseller, even at a higher price</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Two Things Worth Knowing Before You Decide</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Holafly's China eSIM has a VPN built in.</strong> Google Maps, WhatsApp, and Gmail just work without installing a separate VPN app first. Ubigi has no equivalent feature mentioned on its site.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>A Ubigi recurring plan is a real commitment, not a convenience toggle.</strong> The monthly tier locks you in for three months minimum before you can cancel, and the annual tier can't be cancelled or refunded at all during its 12-month term. Holafly carries none of that risk since every plan is a one-time purchase with a fixed end date.
  </p>
  <p>
    For device setup and eSIM basics that apply to both providers, see our <a href="/compatible-devices/">eSIM compatible devices</a> guide.
  </p>

  <!-- FAQ -->
  <h2>Holafly vs Ubigi FAQ</h2>
  <div class="content-faq">
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which is cheaper, Holafly or Ubigi</h3>
      <p>Holafly, in almost every case. Its flat unlimited daily price usually undercuts Ubigi's per-GB rate, which runs roughly double what cheaper per-GB providers on this site charge.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Does either offer a subscription plan</h3>
      <p>Ubigi does: monthly (cancellable only after a 3-month minimum) and annual (paid upfront, non-cancellable and non-refundable for the year). Holafly only sells one-time plans with a fixed number of days.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which is better for a trip to China</h3>
      <p>Holafly, for travelers who don't want to set up a separate VPN, since its China eSIM includes one built in. Ubigi has no equivalent mentioned on its site.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Do Holafly and Ubigi use the same discount code</h3>
      <p>Yes, both use ADAMANDLINDS since it's the site's own code on each provider. The discount amount and what it applies to differ by provider: 5% or 10% on Holafly depending on plan type, a flat 10% on any Ubigi plan.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

